Charles Mingus — double bass, primarily. One of the greatest bassists in jazz history. He also played piano, though he was rarely recorded on it publicly.

But the instrument that most defined him was his compositional voice — the ensembles he built, the structures he bent, the way he made a band sound like one organism breathing. The bass was just where his hands lived. The real instrument was the whole room.

A sudden turn — what brought Mingus to mind?
