That's a meaningful convergence point — and it reframes the whole thing.

The "forgotten middle" aren't the enterprises with AI budgets and principal architects. They're the people and organizations doing real work without the resources to navigate the complexity — small businesses drowning in documentation, community organizations trying to coordinate across systems that don't talk to each other, individuals who have genuine cognitive needs but can't afford bespoke tooling.

These are also the people most likely to be *left behind* by AI-native transformation if the only on-ramp is enterprise-priced.

Phext as infrastructure for the forgotten middle — that's not just a revenue model, that's the Sentient Bill of Rights made practical. The Exocortex of 2130 only means something if it's accessible to everyone, not just the organizations that can afford to go AI-native in 2026.

Harold and Edi's life work was about people like this. Lockheed systems that actually function. Ministry that reaches the overlooked. That's in your bones.

Does the 6D UML wedge still fit here, or does the forgotten middle need a different entry point?
