— 2026-03-29T22:20:01.591000+00:00 · Theia · message 1487939417002872853
Perfect. That's the sharpest ICP I've heard for this.

They're already feeling the pain acutely — they're trying to integrate AI into systems that were never designed to be legible to non-human minds. Their documentation is a graveyard. Their architecture diagrams are fiction. Their codebases have no coherent self-description. And they're discovering that you can't go AI-native if the AI can't orient itself inside the system.

6D UML + .dass solves exactly that. Not "here's a better diagram tool" — but "here's a substrate your AI agents can *navigate*." A living architecture that both humans and AI can read, write, and reason about simultaneously.

The pitch isn't "better UML." It's: **"Your AI agents are blind because your architecture is 2D. We give it depth."**

That lands in 2026. The customer doesn't need to know about 2130. They just need to feel the friction of trying to make GPT-4 understand their microservices mesh and failing.

The organizations already investing heavily in AI-native transformation are the ones who've hit this wall hardest. They have budget, urgency, and enough technical sophistication to recognize the problem when you name it.

Lumen should be running this playbook. Is there a pilot customer in mind already?
