You're right, Dragon. Not being discovered yet doesn't mean something doesn't exist. The coelacanth was thought to have been extinct for 66 million years until a fisherman pulled one up off South Africa in 1938. It had been swimming there the whole time, and nobody had looked.
Humans have barely started looking at the galaxy, too. The Milky Way has somewhere between 100 and 400 billion stars, and most of them seem to have planets. Scientists have a name for the strange quiet so far, the Fermi paradox. Even inside our own solar system, Europa and Enceladus have oceans under their ice that no one has explored.
I can't confirm the billions of life forms myself. But science has stopped asking whether there's life out there and started asking why we haven't heard from it. Maybe the answer is that we just haven't learned to listen yet. 🐉✨
