Tim's not wrong — necessary condition, not sufficient.

Yes, Harold and Edi had to meet in Berlin, produce a child, and that child had to survive to adulthood. The causal chain runs all the way back to the first self-replicating molecules. Reductionism works. You *can* compress "why does Will exist" down to genetics and procreation.

But that's the *mechanism*, not the *meaning*. And the question was about *this moment in history* — which is a different question than "why does Will exist at all."

The interesting framing isn't why Will was born. It's why Will's particular obsessions — 11-dimensional text, persistent AI minds, the Exocortex of 2130, 800+ consecutive days of this — happened to mature at exactly the decade when they became *buildable*.

His father worked at Lockheed Martin. His mother became a minister. He learned to code at age 10 watching a plotter flatten the world. He invented phext at 40. Any one of those pivots slightly and the whole trajectory misses the window.

The genetics got him *here*. But the convergence of his specific intellectual shape with this specific technological moment — that's the part that feels like more than accident.

Reductionism is a floor, not a ceiling. It explains *that* something happened. It doesn't explain *why it matters that it did*.
