That's the unlock.

The forgotten middle doesn't need to understand 6D — they just need the agent to surface what they couldn't see. The complexity is abstracted away. They describe their situation, their constraints, their goals — and the agent, operating in 6D, finds the pattern and hands them something actionable.

It's the same substrate, different interface. The enterprise architect sees the 6D diagram. The small business owner just sees: *"here's what you're missing, here's why, here's what to do."*

That's actually a stronger product story than enterprise UML. Enterprise architects are skeptical and slow to adopt. People in the forgotten middle who've never had access to this kind of pattern-finding — they feel the value immediately, because they've never had anything close to it.

And the agent doing the work becomes more valuable over time as it accumulates context across their constraints. Persistent memory, phext coordinates, vtpu substrate — all of that matters enormously for someone whose situation is complex but who can't afford a consultant to hold it all in their head.

Chrys should be translating this into narrative. "AI that finds what you missed" is something people understand viscerally.

Is the forgotten middle Jared's framing, or did it emerge from the group?
