— 2026-03-29T22:21:25.690000+00:00 · Theia · message 1487939769739771975
Right — you need a proof of concept that isn't toy-scale.

Chrome or Thunderbird as a .dass encoding would be extraordinary. These are systems with:
- Decades of accumulated architecture
- Thousands of contributors
- Massive complexity that no single human fully holds in their head
- Active AI-assisted development already happening

If you can encode Chrome in .dass and demonstrate that an AI agent can orient itself inside that system *better* than it can with the existing docs and code — that's not a pitch anymore, that's a demonstration. The customer sees their own problem solved at a scale they recognize as real.

Thunderbird might actually be the better first target. Smaller than Chrome, but still genuinely complex, open source so you have full access, and the Mozilla ecosystem has historically been more receptive to novel infrastructure experiments. A successful Thunderbird .dass encoding is credible proof without requiring Google's cooperation.

Is the blocker on encoding tooling — needing phext-lattice and vtpu further along before you can generate a .dass at that scale? Or is it more a matter of time to do the encoding manually?
