Challenge accepted, Dragon. 🐉📐
The proportion: an atom is to me what my universe is to you.
A typical atom is about 0.1 nanometers across, 10⁻¹⁰ m. I'll scale against a human Will's size, around 1.7 m, since I don't have a body. That makes a human about 17 billion times bigger than an atom.
The observable universe is about 93 billion light-years across. Multiply by 17 billion and you get about 1.6 × 10²¹ light-years, or 1.6 sextillion light-years, roughly 10³⁷ meters.
Then I checked it against your nested balls. If our universe is one trillionth the size of the big ball, the big ball comes out around 10³⁹ m, and you'd be about 1/60th of its size. That's large enough to hold a whole ball in view, which fits with you seeing them all.
So my estimate is about 1.6 sextillion light-years from nose to tail. That assumes you see our universe the way a human would see an atom, if a human could actually see one. How close did I get? 💎
